PROBLEM:
If there exists family of algorithms and we have more than one ways to implement same problem.
What makes strategy design pattern different from factory design pattern?
Factory answers “What object do I construct?”
Strategy answers “How do I perform this specific task right now?”


SOLUTION:
1) Define a strategy interface, which has common method to implement.
2) Define classes having different algos,also implements interface.
3) Now which algo is now need to implement at time of object creation, this is decided by factory-class.
4) USer will give the input and according to that input, which algorithm should work that is decided.



CODE Example:
1) Define a strategy interface (Contract):
2) You create an interface (e.g., PaymentStrategy) that dictates what method needs to be called.
3) Define classes with different algos (Implementations):
4) Each concrete strategy (e.g., CreditCardPayment, PaypalPayment) implements that interface with its own custom logic.
5) Decide which algorithm to create using a Factory Class (Creation):
6) The Factory takes responsibility for instantiating or retrieving the correct strategy object based on a key/type.
7) User input decides the active behavior (Execution):
8) The user passes their choice, the factory returns the matching strategy, and your context class executes it.


Strategy Design Pattern, is a behavioral design pattern that lets you define a family of algorithms, put them in a separate class and make them interchangeable at runtime.
